(Title) Tj
T*
(Nursing-supported digital intervention for sodium management in progressive chronic kidney) Tj
T*
(disease: feasibility and effectiveness) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: Digital health tools combined with clinical support may enhance lifestyle) Tj
T*
(adherence in chronic kidney disease management. Objective: To assess feasibility,) Tj
T*
(engagement, and outcomes of a nurse-coordinated app-based platform for sodium reduction in) Tj
T*
(patients with stages 3-4 chronic kidney disease. Methods: Ninety-nine patients \(mean age) Tj
T*
(58 years; 54% male\) were randomly assigned to enhanced digital coaching or standard care.) Tj
T*
(The intervention comprised 12 weeks of app-based reminders, educational resources, and) Tj
T*
(weekly nurse consultations. Primary feasibility endpoint was protocol completion rate;) Tj
T*
(secondary endpoints included urinary sodium levels, systolic blood pressure, and dietary) Tj
T*
(knowledge. Results: Eighty-five percent of intervention participants completed 8 or more) Tj
T*
(sessions. Mean sodium excretion declined by 18% in the intervention group versus 3% in) Tj
T*
(controls. Blood pressure reduction favored intervention by 6 mm Hg systolic. Secondary) Tj
T*
(outcome of dietary sodium knowledge improved significantly in both groups \(P < 0.05\), with) Tj
T*
(a larger difference favoring intervention \(P = 0.008\). Conclusion: The nurse-supported) Tj
T*
(digital platform demonstrated feasibility and favorable trends in sodium reduction and) Tj
T*
(knowledge improvement, though long-term behavioral sustainability requires further) Tj
T*
(investigation.) Tj
